  "_style": "Write in Uzbek Latin script only. Use ʻ (U+02BB) in oʻ and gʻ (never ' ` or ‘) and ʼ (U+02BC) for the tutuq belgisi (sunʼiy, maʼlumot). Always address the user with polite “siz” (-ingiz, -ng, -ing: “Saqlang”, “sherigingiz”); address the couple as “ikkalangiz” / “ikkovingiz” or say “birga”; “Bizning” is used only in the couple’s own names such as “Bizning bogʻ”. Text the user sends to the partner (reactions, ping messages, letters) may use warm “sen” (“Seni oʻylayapman”). The partner is always “sherigingiz” (neutral; never yigitingiz/qizingiz, eringiz/xotiningiz); Uzbek has no grammatical gender, so keep sentences neutral and use “u” only when unavoidable, preferably repeating “sherigingiz”. A partner’s name placeholder (%@) is never declined or given suffixes (no “%@ning”, “%@ga”, “%@dan”): rephrase so %@ stands alone, e.g. “Sherigingiz (%@) javob berdi”, “%@ — yangi xabar”, “%@: …”. Quote marks: outer « », inner „ “, only where the English has quotes; ellipsis is the single character …; no space before . , : ; ? !; keep “ · ”, \\n, emoji and placeholders exactly. Numbers: space as thousands separator (12 500), comma for decimals (4,5), percent “75%”; dates day-month-year (“14-noyabr, 2026”, short 14.11.2026), month and weekday names lowercase, 24-hour time (21:30). Buttons are short verbal nouns in -sh/-ish (“Saqlash”, “Yuborish”, “Qayta urinish”, “Maktubni muhrlash”); instructions in sentences use the polite imperative (“qayta urinib koʻring”). Titles, tabs, chips and buttons use sentence case (capital on the first word only) except proper names, Coupley and Premium; write “SI” for AI (sunʼiy intellekt). Plural categories are [\"one\",\"other\"]: nouns after a number stay singular, so both forms are usually identical (“%lld kun”, “%lld ta xotira”). Tone: warm, light, kind, never sugary; natural Uzbek, not word-for-word.",
